[38;2;135;215;175m⠉⠁[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[19B[38;2;135;215;175m⠈⠉[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[19B[38;2;135;215;175m⠀⠙[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[19B[38;2;135;215;255m⠀⠚[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[19B[38;2;135;215;255m⠐⠒[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[19B[38;2;135;215;255m⠒⠂[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[J[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem is a classic number theory theorem. Let me solve it directly. No [0m
[38;2;124;124;124manswer leak detected in the problem statement.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTheorem: Let p be prime. p = x² + 3y² has integer solution iff p = 3 or p ≡ 1 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(mod 3).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe can use the fact that the ring of integers of Q(√-3) is a PID.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe ring of integers of Q(√-3) is Z[ω] where ω = (-1+√-3)/2, a primitive cube [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mroot of unity. This is a PID (in fact Euclidean).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m(⇐) If p = 3: 3 = 0² + 3·1². Done.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf p ≡ 1 (mod 3): We need to show p = x² + 3y².[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Z[ω] is a PID, ω = e^{2πi/3} = (-1+√-3)/2.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The norm N(a+bω) = a² - ab + b² = (a - b/2)² + 3b²/4. Actually let me [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mreconsider the form.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think about the relationship between x² + 3y² and the norm [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mform.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe norm in Z[ω]: N(a + bω) = (a+bω)(a+bω̄) = a² - ab + b² where ω̄ = ω² = (-1-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m√-3)/2.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, but x² + 3y² is different from a² - ab + b². Let me think.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually x² + 3y² = N(x + y√-3) in Z[√-3], but Z[√-3] is not the full ring of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mintegers; the ring of integers is Z[ω] which is larger.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote: x² + 3y² = (x + y√-3)(x - y√-3). And x + y√-3 = x + y(2ω+1) = (x+y) + 2yω.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo elements of the form x + y√-3 correspond to elements a + bω with a = x+y, b =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m2y, i.e., b even.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo x² + 3y² = N((x+y) + 2yω) where the element has even "ω-coefficient".[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe norm form a² - ab + b² with b even: let b = 2y, a = x + y. Then a² - ab + b²[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= (x+y)² - (x+y)(2y) + 4y² = x² + 2xy + y² - 2xy - 2y² + 4y² = x² + 3y². [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo x² + 3y² = N(x + y√-3) = N((x+y) + 2yω), and the elements x + y√-3 are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexactly elements of Z[ω] with even ω-coefficient, i.e., elements of the subring [0m[38;2;124;124;124mZ[√-3].[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow the proof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m(⇒) Suppose p = x² + 3y² for some integers x, y. If y = 0 then p = x², [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mimpossible for prime p unless... p = x² means p is a perfect square, only p [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprime works if... no prime is a perfect square. So y ≠ 0 (unless p=... no). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mActually if y=0, p=x², no prime. So y ≠ 0. Similarly if x = 0, p = 3y², so 3 | [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp, p = 3, y = ±1. That gives p = 3 case.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor general: p = x² + 3y². Mod 3: p ≡ x² (mod 3). So either 3 | x (then p ≡ 0 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmod 3, so p = 3) or x² ≡ 1 mod 3, so p ≡ 1 mod 3. This shows p = 3 or p ≡ 1 mod [0m[38;2;124;124;124m3. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124m(⇐) p = 3: 3 = 0² + 3·1². ✓.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mp ≡ 1 (mod 3): Need to show p = x² + 3y².[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mStep 1: Show -3 is a quadratic residue mod p (for p ≡ 1 mod 3, p ≠ 3).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince p ≡ 1 mod 3, the multiplicative group (Z/pZ)* has order p-1 divisible by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3, so there's an element of order 3, say ζ. Then ζ³ = 1, ζ ≠ 1, so ζ² + ζ + 1 = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m0 mod p. The discriminant of t² + t + 1 is -3, so -3 is a QR mod p. More [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdirectly: ζ satisfies t² + t + 1, roots are (-1 ± √-3)/2, so √-3 exists mod p, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mi.e., -3 is QR mod p.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlternatively: by quadratic reciprocity, (−3/p) = (p/3) (one can show (−3/p) = 1[0m[38;2;124;124;124miff p ≡ 1 mod 3). Let me just use the order-3 element argument which is clean.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo there exists t with t² ≡ -3 (mod p), i.e., p | t² + 3 = (t + √-3)(t - √-3) in[0m[38;2;124;124;124mZ[√-3] ⊂ Z[ω].[0m

